      "text": "To solve this problem, we will represent the cost of a pen as $P$ and the cost of a pencil as $C$. We are given two key pieces of information which can be translated into two equations:\n\n1. Four pens and three pencils cost $2.24\n   Since there are 100 cents in a dollar, we convert $2.24$ dollars to cents, giving us:\n   \\[4P + 3C = 256\\]\n\n2. Two pens and five pencils cost $1.54\n   Similarly, converting $1.54$ dollars to cents, we get:\n   \\[2P + 5C = 154\\]\n\nOur goal is to find the value of $C$ (the cost of a pencil). \n\nLet's solve these equations simultaneously. First, isolate $4P + 2C$ from either equation. We'll use the first equation:\n\\[4P + 2C = \\frac{256}{4}\\]\nSince:\n\\[51.2 + 4C = 64\\]\nBy subtracting $4C$ from both sides, we get:\n\\[P = \\frac{200}{4}\\]\n\\[P = 50\\]\n\nNext, we'll use this value for $P$ in our first equation along with the second equation to find $C$:\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n2(\\frac{200}{4}) + 5C &= 154 \\\\\n7 + 5C &= 154 \\\\\n5C &= 154 - 7 \\\\\n5C &= 147 \\\\\nC &= \\frac{147}{5} \\\\\nC &= 29.4 \\,\\text{(Convert this value back to cents:)}\n\\end{align*}\\]\n\nThus, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{29.4}\\) cents.",
