      "text": "We can set up a system of linear equations to represent the given information:\n4x + 3y = 224 (equation 1)\n2x + 5y = 154 (equation 2)\nwhere x represents the cost of a pen in cents and y represents the cost of a pencil in cents.\nTo solve the system of equations, we can use the method of elimination. Multiplying equation 1 by 2 and equation 2 by 4, we get:\n8x + 6y = 448 (equation 3)\n8x + 20y = 616 (equation 4)\nSubtracting equation 3 from equation 4, we get:\n14y = 168\nDividing both sides by 14, we find:\ny = 12\nSo, the cost of a pencil is $\\boxed{12}$ cents.\n\nHow do you solve sin(θ + π/2)?\n\nTo solve \\(\\sin(\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\), we can use the angle addition formula for sine. The angle addition formula for sine states:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(a + b) = \\sin(a)\\cos(b) + \\cos(a)\\sin(b)\n\\]\n\nIn our case, \\(a = \\theta\\) and \\(b = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2}) = \\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2}) + \\cos(\\theta)\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2})\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to know the values of \\(\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\) and \\(\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\). We know from trigonometric values of common angles:\n\n\\[\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the expression, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2}) = \\sin(\\theta) \\cdot 0 + \\cos(\\theta) \\cdot 1\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2}) = 0 \\cdot \\sin(\\theta) + 1 \\cdot \\cos(\\theta) = \\cos(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified form of \\(\\sin(\\theta + \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\cos(\\theta)}\n\\]\n\nIf $x$ is inversely proportional to another number $y$, with their constant of proportionality being $k$, and we know that $x = 15$ when $y = 4$, determine the value of $x$ when $y = 6$.\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to use the concept of inverse proportionality. If \\(x\\) is inversely proportional to \\(y\\), then we can write the relationship as:\n\n\\[ x \\cdot y = k \\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is the constant of proportionality. We are given that \\(x = 15\\) when \\(y = 4\\). We can use this information to find the value of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[ 15 \\cdot 4 = k \\]\n\\[ k = 60 \\]\n\nNow that we have the constant of proportionality \\(k = 60\\), we can use it to find the value of \\(x\\) when \\(y = 6\\). Substituting \\(y = 6\\) and \\(k = 60\\) into the equation \\(x \\cdot y = k\\), we get:\n\n\\[ x \\cdot 6 = 60 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{60}{6} \\]\n\\[ x = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x\\) when \\(y = 6\\) is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).\n\nTwo congruent cones with radius 12 cm and height 12 cm are enclosed within a cylinder of height 24 cm. What is the area of the curved surface of the cylinder? Express your answer in terms of $\\pi$.\n\nTo find the area of the curved surface of the cylinder, we start by determining its dimensions. The cylinder has a height of 24 cm and a radius of 12 cm. The formula for the surface area of a cylinder, which includes both the bottom and the top, is given by:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi r h + 2\\pi r^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius and \\( h \\) is the height of the cylinder. Substituting the given values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi \\cdot 12 \\cdot 24 + 2\\pi \\cdot 12^2\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the contributions from the two circles (bottom and top) of the cylinder:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi \\cdot 12 \\cdot 24 = 576\\pi\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the contribution from the curved surface of the cylinder:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi \\cdot 12^2 = 2\\pi \\cdot 144 = 288\\pi\n\\]\n\nAdding these two areas together gives the total surface area of the cylinder:\n\n\\[\n576\\pi + 288\\pi = 864\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the curved surface of the cylinder is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{864\\pi}\n\\]",
